  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 244 AM MDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nFor Sunday, high temperatures are forecast in the 50s to 60s. Storm \nchances start Sunday evening with PoPs of 50-75% increasing west to \neast. Precipitation type will likely be rain based on forecast \ntemperatures and cloud cover. Instability is overall weak with \nmodest lapse rates and spotty CAPE values of <500 J/kg, so a weak \nthunderstorm or two isn't out of the question for the eastern county \nwarning area (CWA) if it is able to tap into the weak instability in \nthe environment. \n\nUpper-level features are likely to send us into a more active \nweather pattern Sunday through at least Tuesday. A deep trough is \nforecast to extend from the Pacific Northwest (PNW) southeast with a \nsecond trough not too far behind it in the western Pacific. As these \nfeatures propogate east, it will allow the subtropical jet to drift \nnorth placing a strong jet streak across the region Sunday. Models \nalso show a surface low develop in eastern Colorado Sunday evening \nand propogate east sometime overnight into Monday. Ensembles \ngenerally agree that the low will set up in eastern Colorado, but \ndiverge on when it will traverse east and where it will set up. \nCurrently, ensembles show the surface low setting up somewhere in \nthe Central Plains or the Oklahoma/Texas Panhandle. The interaction, \ntrack, and evolution of these surface and upper-level features will \nplay a major role in our weather pattern heading into the work week. \n\nThe upper-level pattern could support a high wind event Monday and \nTuesday, but the big question will be where the surface low tracks \nand the amount of cloud cover across the CWA. Clear skies will allow \nstronger winds to mix down to the surface while clouds will keep the \nwinds above the surface. If we end up with clear skies and a low \nfurther south or east of the CWA, we could see wind gusts up to 60 \nmph Monday and Tuesday afternoon. Dust would be unlikely for areas \nthat received precipitation over the weekend and current forecast \nlapse rates are unsupportive of dust being lofted. A low tracking \nover the CWA would likely increase precipitation chances and cloud \ncover, overall lowering risk for strong winds. Low confidence in \nwhich scenario will play out. It will heavily depend on the track \nand evolution of the aforementioned upper-level troughs over the \nweekend.",
